lesson:
```
#include <map>

std::map<std::string, int> stock;
stock["maize"] = 40;
stock["beans"] += 12;        // missing keys start at 0, so this is 12
stock.size();                // 2
stock.count("rice");         // 0: not there
stock.erase("beans");

for (auto& [crop, kg] : stock)
    std::cout << crop << ": " << kg << "\n";   // in sorted key order
```

Careful: just *reading* `stock["rice"]` adds rice with 0. To look without adding, use `stock.count("rice")` or `stock.find("rice") != stock.end()`.

Splitting text into words uses a **string stream**:

```
#include <sstream>
std::istringstream in("the cat the");
std::string word;
while (in >> word) { ... }   // "the", "cat", "the"
```

Unreal's map is `TMap<FString, int32>`; Unity's is `Dictionary<string, int>`.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

int main()
{
    std::map<std::string, int> stock;
    stock["maize"] = 40;
    stock["beans"] += 12;
    for (auto& [crop, kg] : stock) std::cout << crop << ": " << kg << "\n";
    std::cout << "rice? " << stock.count("rice") << "\n";
    return 0;
}

